WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.692
This query classifies phone values instead of immediately changing them.

00:00:04.692 --> 00:00:08.447
The case expression first checks whether the phone is null.

00:00:08.447 --> 00:00:12.807
Then it checks whether the value becomes empty after trimming spaces.

00:00:12.807 --> 00:00:18.207
Next, it removes everything except digits and checks for known placeholder values,

00:00:18.207 --> 00:00:21.922
such as a phone number made entirely of zeros or nines.

00:00:21.922 --> 00:00:25.067
Anything else is labeled as a candidate value.

00:00:25.545 --> 00:00:30.662
Notice the word candidate. We have not proven that the phone number is correct.

00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:37.772
We have only determined that it is not obviously missing, blank, or one of our known sentinel values.

00:00:37.772 --> 00:00:42.957
For example, a ten-digit number can still belong to the wrong person.

00:00:42.957 --> 00:00:48.287
Technical validation and real-world correctness are different questions.

00:00:48.287 --> 00:00:52.787
Run the query and compare the phone value with the state assigned to it.

00:00:52.787 --> 00:00:56.987
This is a good example of using SQL to make uncertainty visible.
